  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 3564,
    "dDurationMs": 4356,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "[Music] Super cooling is a phenomenon where water \nor some other substance gets colder than its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7920,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "freezing point without freezing. It can result \nin very cool demonstrations where you smack a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12240,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bottle of liquid water and it freezes solid before \nyour eyes. But water can also be super cooled in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 16880,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a way that makes it impossible to freeze even by \nsmacking it. One reason to research this kind of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21280,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thing is for cryogenics since the ice crystals \nare often what causes damage to cell walls. If  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25120,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can keep the ice crystals from forming, you \ncan have much better luck resuscitating your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28320,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frozen space traveler. The basic reason super \ncooling is possible in the first place is that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the formation of ice crystals in water has two \ncompeting factions. The interior and the surface.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37280,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The interior of ice has less energy than liquid \nwater. So the interior wants to freeze. But the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42320,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface of ice, the boundary between the ice and \nthe liquid water, the surface actually takes more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy to form than if it just stayed liquid. So \nit wants to stay liquid. Who wins? Well, volume  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51680,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a multiple of the radius cubed, but surface \narea is only a multiple of the radius squared.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56400,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which means big things tend to have proportionally \nlarge interiors, while small things tend to have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proportionally large surfaces. So a really really \ntiny ice crystal will have proportionally way more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65440,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface than interior. And the surface's desire \nto melt will win over the interior's desire to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69760,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "freeze. In super cooled water, any really tiny \nice crystals that form melt back into liquid.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74560,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only once you get a large enough ice crystal, and \nthe exact size depends on the temperature. Only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78720,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a large enough ice crystal is the interior \nproportionally big enough that its desire to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "freeze wins out over the surface and the crystal \ncan grow bigger and bigger and bigger. At4°C,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the tipping point is when crystals get larger \nthan around 20 nanome in size, aka about 70 water  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93040,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "molecules across. At colder temperatures, \nthe tipping point is smaller. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96560,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at -10°C, you only need a crystal larger than \naround 10 nanome in size, about 30 molecules  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102080,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "across. This battle between interior and exterior \nis why pure water cooled below the freezing point  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107040,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "won't necessarily freeze right away. It remains \nas a super cooled liquid and has to wait until a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111760,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crystal forms that's bigger than the tipping point \nand can keep growing and freeze the whole bottle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115440,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which can happen by waiting or by cooling the \nwater more so the tipping point crystal size gets  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119440,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smaller. Or if you have other stuff like minerals \nor dust particles in the water that can jump start  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123280,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ice crystal formation by being bigger than the \ntipping point. or if you provide a jolt of energy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127520,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like by smacking the bottle, then a crystal can \nget above the tipping point size and freeze,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131600,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is all well and good, but you can actually \nstop super cooled water from freezing at all. I  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136240,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "made a previous video about how since water \nexpands when it freezes, if you try to freeze  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140000,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "water inside a rigid container, any of it \nthat becomes ice will expand and pressurize  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143440,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the container, eventually stopping the rest of the \nwater from freezing. So, if you have super cooled  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147040,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "water inside a rigid container, there aren't just \nthe two competing factions of interior wanting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151200,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to freeze and surface wanting to melt. There's a \nthird player, pressure. Pressure makes it harder  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155680,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for ice crystals to grow. So pressure, like the \nsurface, wants ice crystals to melt. Obviously,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160160,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you have a large amount of water, a tiny amount \nof it freezing into ice and expanding doesn't  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163680,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressurize the remaining liquid very much. So, a \nlot of ice can form before there's enough pressure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167360,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to matter. Pressure simply doesn't come into the \npicture until way beyond the tipping point size  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170800,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for runaway ice crystal formation. But if you \nhave a tiny amount of water, the pressure can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174880,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be substantially affected by the formation of \neven a single tiny ice crystal. For containers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178720,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that are smaller than a few ten of a micrometer \nin size, instead of a tipping point where once an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182640,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ice crystal gets big enough, it keeps growing and \ngrowing. In this case, the pressure's desire to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186480,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "melt overwhelms the interior's desire to freeze \nfor all crystal sizes. And all ice crystals  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190960,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "want to melt regardless of their size. So, super \ncooled water in a tiny rigid bottle won't freeze  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195840,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no matter how hard you smack it, as long as the \nbottle is tiny enough. For water 1° C below zero,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201280,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the bottle needs to be less than 200 nm long. \nFor water four degrees below zero, the bottle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205840,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "needs to be even smaller, around 50 nmters, \nwhich maybe isn't surprising since for water  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209920,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "four degrees below zero, the tipping point would \nnormally be a crystal around 20 nmters in size,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213600,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which would be a pretty big proportion of the \ncontainer. And if you have super cooled water  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216720,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a tiny rigid bottle, but you really need it to \nbe ice, just remember the prohibition on freezing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220960,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relies on the container being rigid. If you \nopen the bottle so pressure can escape, then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224800,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smack away. This video is entirely supported by \npatrons on Patreon. If you're already supporting,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230240,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thank you so much. And if you're not, please \nconsider it at patreon.com/minutphysics."
    } ]
  } ]
}
